    RowBox[{"U", "(", "2", ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "31b6aa1a-f9e8-473b-98ee-1bb2ff3b107a"],
 " Theory"
}], "Chapter",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9817252719866257`*^9, 3.9817252830795813`*^9}, {
  3.9817272747743263`*^9, 3.9817272766570597`*^9}, {3.981733804916729*^9, 
  3.981733809256349*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"6008a81f-7b28-4bb6-9feb-\
3f635dba4e10"],

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"S", "\[VeryThinSpace]", 
    RowBox[{"U", "(", "2", ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "de4ad8b9-cdde-429d-9287-42c93007f10c"],
 " normally appears in a Physics curriculum for the first time in quantum \
mechanics, applied to spinors. However, its origins are in classical \
mechanics, where it appears as quaternions, specifically to resolve gimbal \
lock. It took a very long time after the realization that no choice of Euler \
angles can prevent gimbal lock to synthesize a concise theory of rotations in \
classical mechanics. ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"S", "\[VeryThinSpace]", 
    RowBox[{"U", "(", "2", ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "702e42ba-af8d-4194-bcaf-e3a478546aaf"],
 " is the culmination of a long chain of discoveries by Euler, Rodrigues, \
Hamilton, Cayley, Klein, Galois, Lie, and others that go through several \
areas of mathematics: topology, geometry, differential equations, and \
algebra. "
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.981412216666863*^9, 3.981412541021722*^9}, {
   3.981453609466157*^9, 3.9814536801716137`*^9}, {3.9814537378941936`*^9, 
   3.9814537387569304`*^9}, {3.9814537716162367`*^9, 3.981453822717458*^9}, {
   3.981503100644874*^9, 3.981503224303117*^9}, {3.981552804526195*^9, 
   3.9815528071383057`*^9}, {3.981552842103586*^9, 3.9815528446605263`*^9}, {
   3.981623807488196*^9, 3.9816238280885572`*^9}, {3.981623863232586*^9, 
   3.981623942003899*^9}, {3.98162401039598*^9, 3.981624016993409*^9}, {
   3.981644075024417*^9, 3.98164421265139*^9}, {3.9817271096931143`*^9, 
   3.9817272670546913`*^9}, {3.981733060088983*^9, 3.981733286923869*^9}, {
   3.981736046711656*^9, 3.981736049185995*^9}, {3.981737256155051*^9, 
   3.981737320038478*^9}, {3.9817373833769093`*^9, 3.9817377020965023`*^9}, {
   3.981738102368638*^9, 3.981738208559224*^9}, 3.9817382908540277`*^9, {
   3.9817494781145897`*^9, 3.981749549919586*^9}, {3.982079714252822*^9, 
   3.982079714738648*^9}, {3.9821494201043987`*^9, 
   3.9821494524557343`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"9909c837-57ef-4b4e-9bc8-\
485844ae3417"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The surprising outcome is that, despite appearances, 3D space requires two \
full rotations\[LongDash]",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"720", "\[Degree]"}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "fb146f1e-e667-4c7a-a537-47e9bab4221d"],
 "\[LongDash]to restore a system to its original topological state, avoiding \
twist relative to the environmant. A single rotation of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"360", "\[Degree]"}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "64fab4ea-23a4-4ace-8a69-926ed04b60b3"],
 " leaves a system in a \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]twisted\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] state, requiring \
another ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"360", "\[Degree]"}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "55b64bfb-718f-4a82-95a3-b4551a19d5eb"],
 "\[LongDash]in the same direction\[LongDash]to undo. This fact can be \
demonstrated by loosely strapping an apparatus to a lab bench and then \
turning the apparatus about a vertical axis. There are numerous other \
demonstrations such as the Dirac belt trick and the Indonesian candle dance. \
There is nothing quantum or abstract about this fact."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.981412216666863*^9, 3.981412541021722*^9}, {
   3.981453609466157*^9, 3.9814536801716137`*^9}, {3.9814537378941936`*^9, 
   3.9814537387569304`*^9}, {3.9814537716162367`*^9, 3.981453822717458*^9}, {
   3.981503100644874*^9, 3.981503224303117*^9}, {3.981552804526195*^9, 
   3.9815528071383057`*^9}, {3.981552842103586*^9, 3.9815528446605263`*^9}, {
   3.981623807488196*^9, 3.9816238280885572`*^9}, {3.981623863232586*^9, 
   3.981623942003899*^9}, {3.98162401039598*^9, 3.981624016993409*^9}, {
   3.981644075024417*^9, 3.98164421265139*^9}, {3.9817271096931143`*^9, 
   3.9817272670546913`*^9}, {3.981733060088983*^9, 3.981733286923869*^9}, {
   3.981736046711656*^9, 3.981736049185995*^9}, {3.981737256155051*^9, 
   3.981737320038478*^9}, {3.9817373833769093`*^9, 3.981737957405755*^9}, {
   3.981738356366674*^9, 3.981738439366407*^9}, {3.981742533386362*^9, 
   3.981742701920815*^9}, 3.981742780462655*^9, {3.9817495853510103`*^9, 
   3.981749596037949*^9}, {3.98214949822014*^9, 3.982149533204302*^9}, {
   3.982157672256053*^9, 
   3.982157692290058*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"2c0e785f-bcd8-4d8b-906f-\
e08cde507255"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "We cannot see this fact directly in ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"S", "\[VeryThinSpace]", 
    RowBox[{"O", "(", "3", ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "fa28c535-77ad-4d57-8076-3bc649a97a7b"],
 " because the Euler angles of the original state and of the twisted state \
are the same. However, in ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"S", "\[VeryThinSpace]", 
    RowBox[{"U", "(", "2", ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "d22ef5ab-02a7-47a2-b23d-60cc71f24a63"],
 ", they are of opposite algebraic signs. The representations of the original \
and twisted states in ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"S", "\[VeryThinSpace]", 
    RowBox[{"U", "(", "2", ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "3b83a76c-f898-4445-b8f1-5deccdb7cef4"],
 " can be smoothly connected by a curve entirely in ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"S", "\[VeryThinSpace]", 
    RowBox[{"U", "(", "2", ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "ef5a86ea-ad8e-4195-9e73-2d109d76f968"],
 ". Such is not possible in ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"S", "\[VeryThinSpace]", 
    RowBox[{"O", "(", "3", ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "b5281093-50de-490f-acb4-99485b5cb09b"],
 ", where to get from one state to the other, we must pass through a \
coordinate singularity, resulting in gimbal lock. ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"S", "\[VeryThinSpace]", 
    RowBox[{"O", "(", "3", ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "ac78388c-7894-4a5c-aca5-01bd674ecdd3"],
 " is literally only half the story of rotation in 3D. Another view is \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]double cover:\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] that a single \
rotation representation in ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"S", "\[VeryThinSpace]", 
    RowBox[{"O", "(", "3", ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "e843cdf6-00da-4eae-b8fc-bc1435b983f3"],
 " has exactly two representations in ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"S", "\[VeryThinSpace]", 
    RowBox[{"U", "(", "2", ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "be965aac-3d01-4e83-a8a2-d441588e942b"],
 ". "
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->CompressedData["
1:eJxTTMoPSmViYGCQAGIQ/TFM9P2HlW8dP21cIP4RSB96pafKuuqt451aVj0Q
bTPXxhREH7OIANNmO4otQXTW12v2IPr8oin+skBaaTNnPIiefm3CFFMgzZD7
BExPCXCeCaI5lKeCad/57+0jgHTZ1SYnEB1R+NwNRH+UPxAAou+t9okE0Uyn
5KNANNuJBNUkIG1wY4oZiP6nu21DF8i8A4lHQPTnG00zeoF02IS7W0D046PX
